---
import { storyEyebrow, storyTitle } from '../data/stories';
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import Reconstruction from '../components/Reconstruction.astro';

const SITE = 'https://alucify.ai/';
const APP = 'https://alucify-screens.fly.dev/#/dashboard';
const FIGMA = 'https://www.figma.com/design/N7vnpK60xZfzI8luYKbevA/Alucify-Patterns?node-id=0-1';
const FIGMA_EMBED =
  'https://embed.figma.com/design/N7vnpK60xZfzI8luYKbevA/Alucify-Patterns?node-id=0-1&embed-host=share';
const W = '/work/alucify';

/** The commercial promise, in the order the site makes it. */
const promise = [
  ['Scope', 'Understand the system'],
  ['Commit', 'Fix the scope, price, and timeline'],
  ['Deliver', 'Build and review the result'],
];

/** The delivery model, in the order the site explains it. AI code comes last. */
const model = [
  ['World model', 'Requirements, schemas, and code are synthesised into a queryable model.'],
  ['Simulation', 'The model is used to identify edge cases, defects, and impact.'],
  ['AI code', 'AI builds against that context, with senior engineers reviewing the work.'],
];

/** The three questions the dashboard answers. */
const review = [
  ['Reach', 'How far did the change travel?'],
  ['Coverage', 'Which decisions did the agent address?'],
  ['Impact', 'Where does a person need to look?'],
];

const severity = [
  ['Blast radius', 'How far the change reaches'],
  ['Regression risk', 'What existing behaviour could break'],
  ['Data integrity', 'What information may be exposed'],
  ['Backward compatibility', 'What existing use may stop working'],
];

const flows = [
  ['First run', ['Sign in', 'Connect source', 'Build context', 'Ready']],
  ['Artifacts', ['Dashboard', 'Artifact', 'Audit', 'Re-audit']],
  ['Coverage', ['Dashboard', 'Coverage summary', 'Decision detail']],
  ['Impact', ['Dashboard', 'Impact summary', 'Concept detail']],
];
---

<DocumentLayout
  title={storyTitle('/alucify')}
  metaTitle="Alucify — designing trust from first visit to code review | Tyler Young"
  description="Alucify builds enterprise software with AI, which creates two moments where a person has to decide whether to trust the model. I designed the site that explains the promise and the product that makes the resulting work reviewable."
  eyebrow={storyEyebrow('/alucify')}
  meta={[
    ['Company', 'AI-native enterprise software delivery'],
    ['Contribution', 'Product design · company website'],
    ['Product scope', '4 flows · 20 documented screens'],
    ['Status', 'Active venture · shown with the founder’s agreement'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    Alucify builds enterprise software with AI. That creates two moments when a
    person has to decide whether to trust the model.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before signing, a buyer has to believe Alucify can commit to scope, price,
    and timeline without giving up enterprise quality.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before code ships, a reviewer has to understand work they did not watch being
    produced: what changed, which decisions were covered, where the risk sits,
    and what the work cost.
  </p>
  <p>
    I designed the public site that explains the promise and the product that
    makes the resulting work reviewable.
  </p>

  <ul class="cells three">
    <li>
      <span class="cell-label">Public website</span>
      <span class="cell-note">The promise</span>
      <a class="artifact" href={SITE} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        Visit alucify.ai <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="cell-label">Product reconstruction</span>
      <span class="cell-note">The review experience</span>
      <a class="artifact" href={APP} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        Open the dashboard <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="cell-label">Complete design file</span>
      <span class="cell-note">The full product system</span>
      <a class="artifact" href={FIGMA} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        Open 20 screens in Figma <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">01 · The trust problem</p>
  <h2>The promise had to be understandable before the technology was</h2>
  <p>
    Alucify’s underlying model is unfamiliar: requirements, schemas, and code
    become a World Model; that model is used to simulate impact and edge cases;
    AI produces the implementation; and a senior engineer reviews every change.
  </p>
  <p>The website could not begin by asking a buyer to understand that architecture.</p>
  <p>It begins with four recognisable business situations:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>An application works but no longer scales</li>
    <li>A prototype needs to become production software</li>
    <li>A legacy system needs modernisation</li>
    <li>A critical feature cannot fit into the current roadmap</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Only after establishing those problems does the site explain how Alucify works.</p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    The story begins with the constraint the buyer recognises, not the technology
    Alucify wants to explain.
  </p>

  <ul class="captures">
    <li>
      <span class="surface-tag">Website · alucify.ai</span>
      <img
        class="capture-shot"
        src={`${W}/site-problem.png`}
        width="1600"
        height="766"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
        alt="The You’re here because section: four pale blue cards reading an app is working but not scaling, a prototype is ready for prime-time, a legacy app needs to be modernized, and a critical new feature needs development."
      />
      <span class="capture-name">You’re here because</span>
      <span class="capture-note">
        The website establishes the buyer’s problem before introducing the World
        Model or the AI delivery process.
      </span>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">02 · The public promise</p>
  <h2>The website reduces the offer to three commitments</h2>
  <p>The commercial model is deliberately simple.</p>

  <ul class="cells three">
    {
      promise.map(([label, note]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="cell-label">{label}</span>
          <span class="cell-note">{note}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p>
    The page reinforces the same promise in three forms: a three-step process, a
    fixed contract, and AI-native delivery with human review on every pull
    request. That makes the unfamiliar delivery method legible through familiar
    commitments.
  </p>

  <ul class="captures">
    <li>
      <span class="surface-tag">Website · alucify.ai</span>
      <img
        class="capture-shot"
        src={`${W}/site-promise.png`}
        width="1600"
        height="978"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
        alt="The green Alucify hero, headed We are the future of enterprise software development, closing on three commitments: a three-step process of scope, commit and deliver; a fixed contract for price, scope and timeline; and AI-native delivery with human review on every PR."
      />
      <span class="capture-name">The commitments</span>
      <span class="capture-note">
        The commercial promise is stated before the site explains the technical
        mechanism behind it.
      </span>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">03 · The model</p>
  <h2>The mechanism is explained as coherence, rigour, and speed</h2>
  <p>
    Once the promise is clear, the site introduces three parts of Alucify’s
    delivery model.
  </p>

  <ul class="cells three">
    {
      model.map(([label, note]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="cell-label">{label}</span>
          <span class="cell-note">{note}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p>
    The sequence matters. AI code appears last. Context and validation establish
    the conditions under which speed can be credible.
  </p>

  <ul class="captures">
    <li>
      <span class="surface-tag">Website · alucify.ai</span>
      <img
        class="capture-shot"
        src={`${W}/site-model.png`}
        width="1600"
        height="544"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
        alt="The How we do it section on a violet field, with three labelled columns: coherence and the World Model, rigor and Simulation, speed and AI Code reviewed by senior engineers."
      />
      <span class="capture-name">How we do it</span>
      <span class="capture-note">
        The website positions AI as the implementation layer inside a larger
        system of context, simulation, and human review.
      </span>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The public site explains why the process should be trustworthy. The product
    has to show a reviewer whether a particular result deserves that trust.
  </p>

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">04 · The review product</p>
  <h2>The dashboard begins with the decision to approve</h2>
  <p>
    The product is not a celebration of how much code the agent produced. It is
    an orientation screen for someone accountable for what happens next.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three areas establish the review: artifacts, meaning what was produced,
    integrated or flagged; coverage, meaning which decisions the work addressed;
    and impact, meaning how far the change reaches and where risk sits.
  </p>
  <p>
    Blast radius, agent hours, and token cost appear beside coverage because each
    can affect the decision to approve the work. Cost is also attached to
    individual changes rather than hidden in a separate billing surface.
  </p>

  <ul class="cells three">
    {
      review.map(([label, q]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="cell-label">{label}</span>
          <span class="cell-note">{q}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <Reconstruction
    route="dashboard"
    label="Dashboard"
    title="Alucify code-review dashboard reconstruction"
    caption="The dashboard places artifact status, coverage, and impact in one review surface."
    eager
  />

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">05 · Traceable coverage</p>
  <h2>A score is only credible if the reviewer can open it</h2>
  <p>
    The dashboard summarises coverage as a percentage. The detailed view exposes
    every decision underneath it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each decision is named, categorised as scope, architecture, or data, and
    marked covered, partial, or not covered. The list sorts incomplete decisions
    first.
  </p>
  <p>
    That changes the role of the percentage. It does not end the review; it
    identifies where the review should begin.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    The summary tells the reviewer where they stand. The rows show why.
  </p>

  <Reconstruction
    route="coverage"
    label="Coverage analysis"
    title="Alucify coverage-analysis reconstruction"
    caption="The sample score is calculated from the 27 decisions beneath it, with incomplete decisions placed first."
  />

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">06 · Explained risk</p>
  <h2>Critical status comes with a reason</h2>
  <p>
    Most of the interface remains visually quiet. Critical impact is the
    exception. Opening a concept reveals a written summary and a consistent
    breakdown across four dimensions.
  </p>

  <ul class="cells four">
    {
      severity.map(([label, note]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="cell-label">{label}</span>
          <span class="cell-note">{note}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p>
    Severity is communicated through a label, a visual marker, and a written
    explanation. The reviewer does not have to infer the reason from colour or a
    single score.
  </p>

  <Reconstruction
    route="impact-detail"
    label="Impact detail"
    title="Alucify impact-detail reconstruction"
    caption="The detail view explains why a concept is critical and lets the reviewer move through the queue without returning to the table."
  />

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">07 · System trust</p>
  <h2>The product also has to say when not to trust it</h2>
  <p>The complete dashboard is only one state in the product.</p>
  <p>
    An analysis may still be running. A result may be empty. An audit may become
    stale after the underlying artifact changes. These moments are not
    implementation leftovers; they determine whether a reviewer understands what
    the product currently knows.
  </p>
  <p>I designed the product to make those limits explicit.</p>

  <h3>When the analysis is still running</h3>
  <p>
    The integration state explains what is happening in the background, how the
    user will know when it is complete, and that they do not need to keep the tab
    open.
  </p>

  <ul class="captures">
    <li>
      <span class="surface-tag">Product · reconstruction</span>
      <img
        class="capture-shot"
        src={`${W}/product-in-progress.png`}
        width="1500"
        height="942"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
        alt="The artifact screen mid-analysis: a banner reading creating context for this artifact, promising an email when it is ready, above artifact status, coverage and impact sections shown as grey placeholder rows."
      />
      <span class="capture-name">Integration in progress</span>
      <span class="capture-note">
        The product explains the wait and gives the user permission to leave.
      </span>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>When the answer is no longer current</h3>
  <p>
    A stale audit does not remain on screen as if it were valid. The product
    states that the result has been deprecated and places the re-audit action
    beside that admission.
  </p>

  <Reconstruction
    route="artifact-reaudit"
    label="Stale audit"
    title="Alucify stale-audit reconstruction"
    caption="The product identifies its own result as outdated and puts the corrective action beside it."
  />

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">08 · The complete system</p>
  <h2>Twenty screens connect four review flows</h2>
  <p>
    The product is structured as four horizontal flows rather than a collection
    of dashboard concepts. The dashboard is the shared hub for the three review
    paths, and the onboarding flow establishes the code context those reviews
    depend on.
  </p>

  <div class="flows">
    <p class="sr-only">
      Four flows. First run goes from sign in, to connect source, to build
      context, to ready. The other three each begin at the dashboard: artifacts
      continues to artifact, then audit, then re-audit; coverage continues to the
      coverage summary and then a decision detail; impact continues to the impact
      summary and then a concept detail.
    </p>
    {
      flows.map(([name, steps]) => (
        <div class="flow" aria-hidden="true">
          <p class="flow-name">{name}</p>
          <ol class="flow-steps">
            {steps.map((step) => (
              <li class:list={['flow-step', { hub: step === 'Dashboard' }]}>{step}</li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <figure class="recon figma">
    <div class="recon-head">
      <span class="recon-label">Complete design file</span>
      <span class="recon-host">20 screens · 4 flows</span>
      <a class="recon-open" href={FIGMA} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        Open in Figma <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </div>
    <div class="recon-viewport figma-viewport">
      <p class="recon-fallback">
        Complete design file — <a href={FIGMA} target="_blank" rel="noopener">open in Figma</a>
      </p>
      <iframe
        src={FIGMA_EMBED}
        title="The Alucify design file — twenty screens across four flows"
        loading="lazy"
        referrerpolicy="no-referrer"
        allowfullscreen></iframe>
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      The complete board contains onboarding, artifact review, coverage, impact,
      and the incomplete states connecting them.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p class="sec-eyebrow">09 · My role</p>
  <h2>I designed the promise and the proof</h2>
  <p>I was the designer on the team that built Alucify.</p>
  <p>
    For the public site, I designed how the company explains the buyer’s problem,
    the fixed-price delivery model, the World Model, simulation, AI
    implementation, human review, and the evidence supporting the offer.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the product, I designed the information architecture, dashboard metrics,
    coverage model, impact and severity system, and the states from first
    connection through daily review.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two surfaces solve different moments in the same decision. Can I trust
    this company to build with AI? Can I trust this AI-generated change enough to
    approve it?
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    The website makes the model understandable. The product makes its work
    inspectable.
  </p>

  <div class="disclosure">
    <p class="sec-eyebrow">About the public work</p>
    <p>The company website is live at alucify.ai.</p>
    <p>
      The product screens are a working reconstruction of my design, built so the
      states can be opened and used rather than viewed only as screenshots. The
      reconstruction is not the shipping product.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tenant, people, and measurements in it are invented. The summaries
      reconcile with their underlying rows, but they are not customer results.
    </p>
    <p>This work is shown with the founder’s agreement.</p>
  </div>
</DocumentLayout>
